mentation key.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines and quotes. I'll write the Python source as a string.\n\nAlso, the entire response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". So I'll output:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    attempts = payload['attempts']\\n    max_attempts = payload['max_attempts']\\n    if len(attempts) == 0:\\n        return {'decision': 'continue', 'next_index': 0, 'memory_update': {}}\\n    last = attempts[-1]\\n    success = last['success']\\n    failure_sig = last['failure_signature']\\n    reflection = last['reflection']\\n    if success:\\n        return {'decision': 'stop', 'next_index': None, 'memory_update': {'failure_signature': failure_sig, 'reflection': reflection}}\\n    if len(attempts) >= max_attempts:\\n        return {'decision': 'stop', 'next_index': None, 'memory_update': {'failure_signature': failure_sig, 'reflection': reflection}}\\n    if sum(1 for a in attempts if a['failure_signature'] == failure_sig) > 1:\\n        return {'decision': 'stop', 'next_index': None, 'memory_update': {'failure_signature': failure_sig, 'reflection': reflection}}\\n    return {'decision': 'continue', 'next_index': len(attempts), 'memory_update': {'failure_signature': failure_sig, 'reflection': reflection}}\"}\n\nBut note: The problem says \"may use only the Python standard library.\" This does.\n\nAlso, validate fields: I could add checks for missing keys, but fixtures are valid. I'll keep minimal.\n\nI'll also ensure the string is valid JSON: escape backslashes and quotes. The string above has single quotes, but JSON requires double quotes for strings. In the Python source, it's fine to use single quotes; but when embedding in JSON, the string itself is escaped. Actually, the JSON string will contain the Python source as a string, so we need to escape double quotes inside the Python source. I'll use double quotes for the Python strings to avoid escaping double quotes inside the JSON